[SetUp "1"]

{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 1. Be2 ( 1. Bxh5 { Alternative for comparison; read the explanation before treating it as a recommendation. } 1... gxh5 ) 1... Kf8 { Illustrative line. The h5-pawn attacks g4. A pawn does not attack the square directly ahead of it; trace its two diagonal capture squares. Bxh5 is met by ...gxh5. The g6-pawn protects h5, so White exchanges a bishop for a pawn and has not saved the piece. Be2 is a safe example. The path g4-f3-e2 is clear, and no black piece attacks e2. Bd1 or Bh3 can also be considered if you verify their paths and destinations; this is not a one-move puzzle. } *

[Event "Stop Hanging Pieces / 01.2A"]
[Site "Original teaching position"]
[Date "2026.09.10"]
[Round "01.2"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "3r2k1/p4ppp/1pn5/8/8/5N1P/PP3PP1/2R3K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ An undefended piece can be a real opportunity. The useful habit is not "never take"; it is "take only after checking defenders and forcing replies." } 1. Rxc6 Rd1+ 2. Kh2 { Illustrative line. Rxc6 wins a knight. Before playing it, look for Black's strongest forcing idea: ...Rd1+. The white king can step to h2 because the h-pawn stands on h3 and h2 is not attacked. The sample line stops after the check is answered. White keeps the extra knight; there is still a game to play. A capture can be sound even when the opponent replies with check, provided you can answer the check without returning the gain. } *

[Event "Stop Hanging Pieces / 01.2B"]
