{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 1. Rc3 { Illustrative line. The black b-pawn is on b7, not b6. From b7 it attacks c6, so the knight now has a pawn defender. Rxc6 bxc6 trades a rook for a knight. Using rough values of rook 5 and knight 3, White gives up about two points of material. These values are guides, but there is no compensating attack in this exercise. Rc3 is one safe example: the rook stays on the c-file and avoids the defended knight. Rc2 and a king move can also be reasonable. The required answer is the defender and the consequence, not a unique quiet move. } ( 1. Rxc6 { Alternative for comparison; read the explanation before treating it as a recommendation. } 1... bxc6 ) *

[Event "Stop Hanging Pieces / 01.3A"]
[Site "Original teaching position"]
[Date "2026.09.10"]
[Round "01.3"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p2Q/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Development is valuable only if it meets the position's immediate demands. An early queen attack is best answered by identifying the mating square and the piece supporting it. } 3... g6 ( 3... Nf6 { Alternative for comparison; read the explanation before treating it as a recommendation. } 4. Qxf7# ) 4. Qf3 Nf6 { Illustrative line. Black begins with ...g6, interrupting the queen's route to f7 while gaining time against the queen. After Qf3, the queen threatens f7 down the f-file instead. Now ...Nf6 develops a piece and blocks that new line. The same developing move that failed on the first turn works on the second because the attack's geometry has changed. Always reassess after the queen moves. } *

[Event "Stop Hanging Pieces / 01.3B"]
