[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p2Q/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Exercise. Try the questions in the book before reading this line. } 3... Nf6 ( 3... Nf6 { Alternative for comparison; read the explanation before treating it as a recommendation. } 4. Qxf7+ Kxf7 ) 4. Qe2 Bc5 { Illustrative line. White's bishop is on b5 instead of c4. It no longer protects f7. The queen's attack looks similar, but the mating support is absent. Yes. After ...Nf6 Qxf7+ Black can play ...Kxf7. The king is not moving into any white attack on f7. White loses a queen for a pawn; the check is not mate. ...Bc5 develops the f8-bishop and eyes f2. Other sensible developing moves exist. The lesson is to inspect the actual support for a threat before importing a familiar response. } *
